  "query_preview": "..\nInvest 90W's influence continues to bring unsettled weather to the\nMarianas. A Mid-Level Circulation (MLC) is apparent to the \nsoutheast, producing the convective outbursts encroaching upon the \nregion from the east. As 90W continues to consolidate (discussed in \nthe Tropical Systems section below), the moisture influx will persist\ndespite the center remaining well away from the islands. CIMSS MIMIC\nindicates precipitable water (PWAT) values around 2.5 inches, with \nsome pockets reaching up to 3.0 inches to the east. Vorticity and \nupper-level divergence continues to be favorable for convection, so \nisolated thunderstorms are not expected to let up, and locally heavy \nshowers are possible at least for tonight for all islands. Since \nthere are slight spatial and intensity differences in shower coverage\nas well as wind directions and speeds, the zones remained split. As \nfor the long-term, the monsoon trough will trail behind 90W and move \nover the Marianas should 90W lift to the northwest. Model guidance \nindicates this effect to be delayed, however, so showers and \nincreasing winds may be concentrated over Guam and Rota before \nincluding Saipan and Tinian sometime over the weekend. Due to the \nuncertainty regarding timing and direction of 90W's evolution, \nshowers remain scattered with slight chances of thunderstorms for all\nislands. This may be refined as confidence naturally increases with \ntime.",
